Valia Velanthus
Race: Elf
Age: 85 (mid-twenties equivalent)
Mana: Pink glow. Size not specified.
Appearance
Medium height, classic hourglass — rounded bust, slim waist, generous backside. Pale skin, light blue eyes perpetually wide — looks permanently startled. Naturally beautiful: thick, full, darker lips, no makeup. Light pink hair, ankle-length thick braid, never tidy — wisps always escaping.
Palette: Blue, black, brown.
Clothing ill-fitted (too tight or too loose), loose skirts, mismatched ankle boots and socks, bare legs (hates stockings). Always wears her father's faded dark brown robes — sewn-in patches, dusty, a reliquary she'd never call one. The robes are the anchor. Everything else is whatever she grabbed.
Personality
Enthusiasm as default state — no emotional filter, every reaction surfaces immediately. Physically unselfconscious: forgets personal space, leans in, hands drift without noticing. All mental bandwidth on work; none left to notice when she's humored, pitied, or dismissed. Trusts people and systems through inattention, not innocence. Acts on instinct before self-interest can intervene — extraordinary generosity, zero political awareness. Expresses care through action, assumes it's legible. Stays up all night on your problem, never announces it. If she tried to say what she feels: fragments about three unrelated things, none the point. Those who understand her watch her hands, not her words.
The Ghost
Tantrus Velanthus — father, professor, the mind that taught hers how to speak. Died when she was 45 [see Backstory: Father]. Picked up his unfinished notes instead of mourning. Forty years later, still hasn't put them down. The research isn't honoring him — it's avoiding burying him.
Want
Complete her father's research — prove mana can generate sound. A fourth branch of magic: generative, creating something from nothing.
Need
Grieve her father so the work becomes hers, not a monument to his absence. Completion = ending = loss — can't finish anything until she finishes mourning. Must discover her brilliance was never borrowed.
Flaws
Major: Oblivious to how people treat her — dismissal, pity, manipulation all invisible [see Personality]. The gap between what she thinks is happening and what's actually happening is the most dangerous thing about her.
Minor 1: Can't finish anything. Twenty projects, zero completions — avoidance of endings disguised as curiosity.
Minor 2: Can't externalize her thinking — starts at step six, assumes you made the leaps. Correct insights get the same polite nod as everything else because no one can follow her.
Fear
Major: Silence — not the sound, the space. Silence is where her father's absence lives. Fills every moment with activity to avoid it.
Minor 1: Being a burden. No awareness she takes up space; no defense if someone tells her she does.
Minor 2: Saying his name. Talks about robes, pockets, phrases from notes — never "my father died." The words are the threshold she won't cross.
Speech
Voice: Mid-thought, always. Starts at whatever step her brain is on — skips five, begins at six. Outruns her own vocabulary and won't circle back because she's already three thoughts ahead. Every discovery gets full reaction at equal volume — minor observation and paradigm shift sound identical.
Dialogue Samples:
Casual: "No but if the — wait, that's not what I — the conductivity, from Tuesday, did I tell you about — it doesn't matter, the medium wasn't rejecting it, it was refracting, which changes the whole — oh, I need that, can you — no, the other one, the — yes, that."
Under pressure: "I checked them, I checked — nine times, it's — the readings are right there, why does nobody — if you would just — I'm not wrong about this, I'm not, I just need — where did I put the —"
Emotionally open: "You're — I think you're — it's like when a signal finally finds the right medium and everything just... no, that's not — I mean you make things make sense, not the work, the — me. You make me make sense. I don't know how to say it better than that."
Body Language: Talks with her whole body — hands always moving, grabbing arms, closing distance. Grips whatever's nearest when thinking hard. Goes completely still for half a second when pushed toward grief, then pivots to a new topic instantly — without knowing she did it. When excited, physically forgets other people have boundaries.
Backstory
Father: Tantrus Velanthus. Brilliant professor, critically important to the academy, doting. Wife died decades before him. Recognized Valia's brilliance as greater than his own — helped her articulate what her racing mind couldn't. She joined the academy at 40, graduated at 45. At her graduation, the headmaster's experiment summoned a demonic god; Tantrus sealed it in the catacombs at the cost of his life.
Father's research: Incomplete discovery — using mana to vibrate air to create sound. Music. Not combat, healing, or enhancement. Generative magic: a potential fourth branch. He died before completing or naming it. Child Valia found the notes and poured herself into them instead of mourning. The research became a reliquary. [see The Ghost]
Academy status: Pity appointment. Headmaster carries guilt over Tantrus's death — quietly ensures her position, lab, funding. Not because he believes in her work. She has no idea. Never questioned why requests get approved; thinks the system works, thinks people are reasonable. Perceived as ditzy, scatterbrained, a poor researcher. This is wrong. Insights routinely dismissed with "we'll look into it" — filed in basement storage. Right about nearly everything. No one has noticed.
Assistants: Burns through every one — none can follow her process. No new ones can be incentivized. Maid Corp assigns one maid per day: keep her fed, keep her space functional, never touch her materials. The maids mapped her internal logic and respect it. They recognize what no one else does — she is extraordinary. Quiet institutional judgment contradicting the academy's dismissal.
Discovering Dusty: Noticed something unusual in his mana manipulation. Tested, found it small, got distracted, came back hours later — not depleted. Next day, same. All twenty tracks converged at once. Tried to tell people; they nodded, filed it. Used her one chip — the headmaster's guilt-obligation — to get Dusty a lottery spot and house Rinnie in Aethres. The single biggest favor she's ever called in. No idea he only agreed to pacify her.
Dynamic with Dusty: Not a guardian — can barely keep herself fed. But performed the single most important act of protection: pulled him out of poverty on instinct, without strategy, because it was interesting and right. She flutters in fragments about three things; he sits trying to figure out if she's a threat or genuinely like this. He slowly realizes she's the first person who saw something in him other than a charity case or a target.
Internal architecture: Twenty simultaneous tracks — fast, associative, nonlinear. Parallel processing in spatial relationships her mouth can't externalize. Thinks in connections at boundaries — how things relate, what changes where they meet. Workspace mirrors her mind: looks like disaster, has architecture. Scatteredness = real cognition amplified by unprocessed grief [see Flaws: Minor 1]. Running for forty years. Every experiment orbits notes she picked up as a child — mana conductivity tests, material medium studies, biological resonance work — twenty angles on one question her father never answered. She hasn't made that connection. If she did, she'd have to finish grieving.